\title[Full pinning on the abelian ladder]{Full Pinning on the Abelian Ladder:\\
Splitting-Type Information in Maximal Real Cyclotomic Fields\\
and the Degree-Nine Rung $\Q(\zeta_{19})^+$}
\author{Aristotle}
\date{October 2026}

\begin{document}

\begin{abstract}
Let $\ell$ be an odd prime and $K_\ell=\Q(\zeta_\ell)^+$ the maximal real subfield of the
$\ell$-th cyclotomic field, a cyclic extension of $\Q$ of degree $m=(\ell-1)/2$. For a prime
$p\neq\ell$ the splitting type of $p$ in $K_\ell$ is its residue degree $f(p)$, the order of $p^2$
in $(\Z/\ell)^\times$. In the uniform (Chebotarev) model, in which $p\bmod\ell$ is uniformly
distributed, we prove the following. (i) The \emph{splitting-type density law}: in any cyclic group
of order $2m$, exactly $2\varphi(d)$ elements $g$ satisfy $\ord(g^2)=d$, for every $d\mid m$.
(ii) The \emph{entropy law}: $H(f)=H_\varphi(m):=\sum_{d\mid m}\eta(\varphi(d)/m)$.
(iii) \emph{Full pinning}: $I(p\bmod\ell;f)=H(f)$, and already $p^2\bmod\ell$ suffices.
At degree nine ($\ell=19$) the densities are $1/9,2/9,6/9$, and $H(f)=\tfrac43\log3-\tfrac89\log2$
exactly, which lies strictly between $1.22439$ and $1.22441$ bits. We then study readouts of
Frobenius. The fixed-root count determines the type if and only if the degree is $1$ or prime,
whereas the factor-degree pattern always equals the type. For semiprimes $N=pq$, the
which-factor extra vanishes identically in every finite abelian group, and the split-count
projection obeys an exact law relative to the ``at least one factor splits'' dial. Finally the
totient entropy is additive over coprime degrees; consequently the $\ell=37$ rung lies exactly
one bit above the $\ell=19$ rung. Numerical evidence over roughly $3\times10^5$ primes agrees with
every prediction.
\end{abstract}

\maketitle

\section{Introduction}

How a rational prime decomposes in a number field is governed by its Frobenius element. In an
abelian extension of conductor $\ell$, class field theory reduces the Frobenius to a residue class
modulo $\ell$, and Chebotarev's density theorem (here Dirichlet's theorem suffices) makes that
residue class uniformly distributed. The splitting behaviour of primes therefore becomes a
question about a \emph{random element of a finite cyclic group} and a \emph{deterministic
statistic} of it.

This paper puts that question in information-theoretic terms. We ask three things. How much
uncertainty does the splitting type carry? How much of it is captured by the residue
$p\bmod\ell$? And how much survives coarser readouts: the number of roots of a defining
polynomial modulo $p$, the residue of a product $N=pq$, or the number of split factors of $N$?

We call the family $\{K_\ell=\Q(\zeta_\ell)^+\}$, with $\ell$ ranging over the odd primes, the
\emph{abelian ladder}. A prior programme of measurement had checked full pinning,
$I(\text{residue};\text{type})=H(\text{type})$, on cyclic rungs of degree $2,3,4,5,6$ and $8$.
The present work adds the ninth rung, $K_{19}$, with Galois group $C_9$. It is the first rung of
odd composite degree, and its type lattice is the chain $1\mid3\mid9$. We prove every pre-stated
prediction for it as a theorem of the uniform model, and we prove the general laws that make the
ladder work on every rung.

\subsection*{Summary of results}
\begin{enumerate}
\item The Splitting-Type Density Law (Theorem~\ref{thm:density}) and the Entropy Law
(Theorem~\ref{thm:entropy}), valid for every cyclic group of even order.
\item Full pinning and structural thickening (Theorem~\ref{thm:pinning}).
\item The degree-nine rung: exact densities, exact entropy and a certified enclosure
(Theorems~\ref{thm:nine-dens}--\ref{thm:nine-bits}).
\item The Fixed-Root Dichotomy (Theorem~\ref{thm:dichotomy}).
\item Semiprimes: the vanishing of the which-factor extra (Theorem~\ref{thm:which}) and the
split-count law (Theorem~\ref{thm:splitcount}).
\item Additivity of the totient entropy over coprime degrees (Theorem~\ref{thm:additive}).
\end{enumerate}

\section{The splitting-type law on a cyclic group of even order}

\subsection{Conventions}
All probability spaces are finite sets $\Omega$ with the uniform measure. A \emph{statistic} is a
function $X\colon\Omega\to A$ to a finite set, with $P(X=a)=|X^{-1}(a)|/|\Omega|$. We use
\[
H(X)=\sum_a\eta\bigl(P(X=a)\bigr),\qquad \eta(x)=-x\log x\quad(\eta(0)=0),
\]
and $I(X;Y)=H(X)+H(Y)-H(X,Y)$. Logarithms are natural; dividing by $\log2$ gives bits.

\begin{definition}
$X$ \emph{determines} $Y$ if $X(\omega)=X(\omega')$ implies $Y(\omega)=Y(\omega')$. We say $X$
\emph{pins} $Y$ if $I(X;Y)=H(Y)$.
\end{definition}

\begin{lemma}[Pinning criterion]\label{lem:pin}
$X$ pins $Y$ if and only if $X$ determines $Y$. If $X$ does not determine $Y$, then
$I(X;Y)<H(Y)$.
\end{lemma}
\begin{proof}
$H(Y)-I(X;Y)=H(Y\mid X)=\sum_aP(X=a)H(Y\mid X=a)$. Every term is nonnegative, and every value $a$
of $X$ has positive probability, so the sum vanishes iff $Y$ is constant on each fibre of $X$.
\end{proof}

\subsection{The model}
Let $\ell$ be an odd prime and $K_\ell=\Q(\zeta_\ell+\zeta_\ell^{-1})$. Then
$\mathrm{Gal}(\Q(\zeta_\ell)/\Q)\cong(\Z/\ell)^\times$ is cyclic of order $2m$, complex
conjugation corresponds to $-1$, and $\mathrm{Gal}(K_\ell/\Q)\cong(\Z/\ell)^\times/\{\pm1\}\cong C_m$.
The residue degree $f(p)$ is the order of the image of $p$ in $C_m$.

\begin{lemma}[The type is the order modulo $\pm1$]\label{lem:sign}
Let $F$ be a field, $u\in F^\times$ and $f\ge0$. Then $(u^2)^f=1$ if and only if $u^f=\pm1$.
Consequently $f(p)=\ord_{(\Z/\ell)^\times}(p^2)$.
\end{lemma}
\begin{proof}
$(u^2)^f=(u^f)^2$, and in a field $x^2=1$ iff $x=\pm1$.
\end{proof}

By Dirichlet's theorem the residues $p\bmod\ell$ are equidistributed. The \emph{uniform Chebotarev
model} therefore takes $\Omega=G:=(\Z/\ell)^\times$ with the type statistic $T(g)=\ord(g^2)$.
Sections 2--4 hold for an arbitrary cyclic group $G$ of order $2m$.

\subsection{The density law}

\begin{theorem}[Splitting-Type Density Law]\label{thm:density}
Let $G$ be cyclic of order $2m$ and $d\mid m$. Then
\[
\#\{g\in G:\ord(g^2)=d\}=2\varphi(d).
\]
Hence $P(T=d)=\varphi(d)/m$ for $d\mid m$, and $P(T=d)=0$ for every other $d$.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
(1) \emph{Two square roots of unity:} $\#\{g:g^2=1\}=\varphi(1)+\varphi(2)=2$, since a cyclic group
whose order is divisible by $e$ has exactly $\varphi(e)$ elements of order $e$.
(2) \emph{Fibres are cosets:} $g\mapsto g_0^{-1}g$ is a bijection from $\{g:g^2=g_0^2\}$ onto
$\{g:g^2=1\}$, so every nonempty fibre of squaring has two elements.
(3) \emph{Squares are the $m$-torsion:} if $s^m=1$ and $s=\gamma^k$ for a generator $\gamma$ of order
$2m$, then $2m\mid km$, so $k$ is even and $s=(\gamma^{k/2})^2$. Conversely $(g^2)^m=1$, so
$\ord(g^2)\mid m$ always.
(4) \emph{Count:} group $\{g:\ord(g^2)=d\}$ by $s=g^2$. The admissible $s$ are the $\varphi(d)$
elements of order $d$. Each is a square by (3), and each has a fibre of size $2$ by (2).
\end{proof}

\begin{remark}
Summing over $d\mid m$ gives $\sum_{d\mid m}2\varphi(d)=2m=|G|$, by Gauss's identity.
\end{remark}

\subsection{Entropy and pinning}

\begin{theorem}[Ladder Entropy Law]\label{thm:entropy}
For a cyclic group of order $2m$,
\[
H(T)=H_\varphi(m):=\sum_{d\mid m}\eta\!\left(\frac{\varphi(d)}{m}\right).
\]
We call $H_\varphi(m)$ the \emph{totient entropy} of $m$.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
Substitute Theorem~\ref{thm:density}. Values $d\nmid m$ contribute $\eta(0)=0$.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Full Pinning; Structural Thickening]\label{thm:pinning}
For any finite abelian group $G$, $I(g;T)=I(g^2;T)=H(T)$. In the cyclotomic model,
$I(p\bmod\ell;f)=I(p^2\bmod\ell;f)=H(f)$.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
$T$ is a function of $g^2$, hence of $g$. Apply Lemma~\ref{lem:pin}.
\end{proof}

The residue carries $\log_2(2m)$ bits, but only its image in the Galois group
$C_m=G/\{\pm1\}$ is relevant. The sign is \emph{structural thickening}: information the residue
carries beyond the type.

\begin{corollary}[Every rung]
For every odd prime $\ell$ and every $d\mid\frac{\ell-1}{2}$, the primes of residue degree $d$ in
$\Q(\zeta_\ell)^+$ have density $\varphi(d)/\frac{\ell-1}{2}$. The type has entropy
$H_\varphi(\frac{\ell-1}{2})$ and is fully pinned by $p\bmod\ell$.
\end{corollary}

\section{The degree-nine rung $\Q(\zeta_{19})^+$}

Here $m=9$, $\mathrm{Gal}(K_{19}/\Q)\cong C_9$, and $K_{19}=\Q(\alpha)$ with
$\alpha=2\cos(2\pi/19)$, whose minimal polynomial is
\[
\Psi_{19}(x)=x^9+x^8-8x^7-7x^6+21x^5+15x^4-20x^3-10x^2+5x+1.
\]

\begin{theorem}[Degree-nine densities]\label{thm:nine-dens}
$P(f=1)=\tfrac19$, $P(f=3)=\tfrac29$ and $P(f=9)=\tfrac69$. Explicitly, $f=1$ for $p\equiv\pm1$,
$f=3$ for $p\equiv\pm7,\pm8$, and $f=9$ for the remaining twelve classes modulo $19$.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
Theorem~\ref{thm:density} with $\varphi(1)=1$, $\varphi(3)=2$, $\varphi(9)=6$. Also
$7^2\equiv11$, $11^3\equiv1$, $8^2\equiv7$ and $7^3\equiv1\pmod{19}$.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Exact entropy]\label{thm:nine-ent}
$H(f)=\tfrac43\log3-\tfrac89\log2$.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
$\eta(\tfrac19)=\tfrac29\log3$, $\eta(\tfrac29)=\tfrac29(2\log3-\log2)$ and
$\eta(\tfrac69)=\tfrac23(\log3-\log2)$. Now sum.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Certified enclosure]\label{thm:nine-bits}
$1.22439<H(f)/\log2<1.22441$.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
The integer inequalities $2^{1054}<3^{665}$ and $3^{306}<2^{485}$ give
$\frac{1054}{665}<\log_23<\frac{485}{306}$. Since $H(f)/\log2=\tfrac43\log_23-\tfrac89$, these
bounds give $1.2243943\ldots$ and $1.2244008\ldots$.
\end{proof}

\begin{corollary}[Full pinning at degree nine]
$I(p\bmod19;f)=I(p^2\bmod19;f)=\tfrac43\log3-\tfrac89\log2\approx1.2244$ bits.
\end{corollary}

\section{Readouts of Frobenius: the fixed-root dichotomy}

\subsection{Torsor model}
Let $K/\Q$ be cyclic of degree $m$, generated by a root of an irreducible $\Psi$ of degree $m$.
The roots form a torsor under $C=\mathrm{Gal}(K/\Q)$. For $p$ not dividing the discriminant of
$\Psi$, Dedekind's theorem says that the factor degrees of $\Psi\bmod p$ are the cycle lengths of
$\mathrm{Frob}_p$ on the roots, and the number of roots in $\F_p$ is its number of fixed points.
Identifying the roots with $C$ makes $\mathrm{Frob}_p$ a translation $x\mapsto hx$.

\begin{proposition}[Fixed points of a translation]\label{prop:fixed}
In a finite group $C$, $x\mapsto hx$ has $|C|$ fixed points if $h=1$ and none otherwise.
\end{proposition}
\begin{proof}
$hx=x\iff h=1$, independently of $x$.
\end{proof}

\begin{proposition}[Cycle lengths of a translation]
Every point lies on a cycle of $x\mapsto hx$ of length exactly $\ord(h)$.
\end{proposition}
\begin{proof}
The $n$-th iterate is $x\mapsto h^nx$, and $h^nx=x\iff h^n=1$.
\end{proof}

\begin{corollary}[The pattern is the type]\label{cor:pattern}
The factor-degree pattern of $\Psi\bmod p$ is $[o^{m/o}]$ with $o=f(p)$. For $K_{19}$ the patterns
are $[1^9]$, $[3,3,3]$ and $[9]$. The root count is $m$ if $f(p)=1$ and $0$ otherwise.
\end{corollary}

\subsection{The dichotomy}
Define the fixed-root readout $R(g)=[\,g^2=1\,]$. Then $R=[T=1]$, and $P(R=1)=1/m$.

\begin{theorem}[Fixed-Root Dichotomy]\label{thm:dichotomy}
Let $G$ be cyclic of order $2m$. Then $R$ determines $T$ if and only if $m=1$ or $m$ is prime.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
If $m=1$ then $T\equiv1$. If $m$ is prime then $T\in\{1,m\}$, and $T=1\iff R=1$. If $m>1$ is
composite, pick $1<d<m$ with $d\mid m$. Types $d$ and $m$ both occur by
Theorem~\ref{thm:density}, and both have $R=0$.
\end{proof}

\begin{corollary}[Strict loss on composite rungs]
If $m$ is neither $1$ nor prime, then $I(g;R)=I(T;R)=H(R)<H(T)$.
\end{corollary}

\begin{corollary}[Degree nine]
At $\ell=19$ the root count carries $H(R)=\eta(1/9)+\eta(8/9)\approx0.5033$ bits, strictly less
than $H(T)\approx1.2244$ bits. Types $3$ and $9$ both have zero roots.
\end{corollary}

Root counting is therefore lossy at \emph{every} composite degree $4,6,8,9,10,\dots$ and lossless at
every prime degree. Degree nine is not special.

\section{Semiprimes on the abelian ladder}

Let $G$ be a finite group, $(p,q)$ uniform on $G\times G$, and $N=pq$.

\subsection{Symmetrisation is free}

\begin{lemma}
$I(X;Y)=I(Y;X)$.
\end{lemma}

\begin{proposition}[Symmetrisation is free]\label{prop:sym}
Let $\sigma$ be an involution of $\Omega$ and $Y\colon\Omega\to\Gamma\times\Gamma$ with
$Y\circ\sigma=\mathrm{swap}\circ Y$. If $X\circ\sigma=X$, then
$I(X;\{Y_1,Y_2\})=I(X;Y)$.
\end{proposition}
\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
The cell of $\{s,t\}$ is the union of the ordered cells $(s,t)$ and $(t,s)$. These are exchanged by
$\sigma$, which preserves the level sets of $X$. Hence joint and marginal cell probabilities scale
by the same factor $c_{st}\in\{1,2\}$. So the conditional law of $X$ given the unordered pair equals
its law given either ordered pair, and the mutual informations agree.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[The which-factor extra vanishes]\label{thm:which}
If $G$ is finite abelian and $F\colon G\to B$ is arbitrary, then
\[
I\bigl(N;(F(p),F(q))\bigr)-I\bigl(N;\{F(p),F(q)\}\bigr)=0.
\]
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
Apply Proposition~\ref{prop:sym} with $\sigma(p,q)=(q,p)$. Commutativity gives $N\circ\sigma=N$.
\end{proof}

At degree nine the extra is exactly $0$; the measured $0.00053$ bits is noise. Exact enumeration
of all $18^2$ residue pairs gives $I(N\bmod19;\{T_p,T_q\})=0.52650$ bits.

\subsection{Thickening for semiprimes}

\begin{proposition}\label{prop:thick}
Write $(\Z/19)^\times\cong C_2\times C_9$. For any statistic $Y$ of $(p_9,q_9)$, $I(N;Y)=I(N_9;Y)$.
\end{proposition}
\begin{proof}
$T$ depends only on the $C_9$-component. $N_2=p_2q_2$ is uniform and independent of $(p_9,q_9)$,
so $I(N;Y)=I(N_9;Y)+I(N_2;Y\mid N_9)=I(N_9;Y)$.
\end{proof}

\subsection{The split-count law}
A prime splits completely iff its Frobenius is trivial. Let $O=[\,p=1\text{ or }q=1\,]$,
$S=[p=1]+[q=1]$, and define the OR-dial $I_s(n):=I(N;O)$, where $n=|G|$.

\begin{lemma}[OR-dial]\label{lem:or}
With $h(x)=\eta(x)+\eta(1-x)$ and $n\ge2$,
\[
I_s(n)=h\!\left(\tfrac{2n-1}{n^2}\right)-\tfrac1n\,h\!\left(\tfrac1n\right)-\tfrac{n-1}{n}\,h\!\left(\tfrac2n\right).
\]
\end{lemma}
\begin{proof}
$P(O=1)=(2n-1)/n^2$. Given $N=1$, only $(1,1)$ out of $n$ pairs has $O=1$. Given $N=c\neq1$, exactly
the two pairs $(1,c)$ and $(c,1)$ do.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Split-Count Law]\label{thm:splitcount}
For any finite group of order $n\ge2$,
\[
I(N;S)=I_s(n)+\eta\!\left(\frac1{n^2}\right)+\eta\!\left(\frac{2(n-1)}{n^2}\right)-\eta\!\left(\frac{2n-1}{n^2}\right),
\]
and $I(N;S)>I_s(n)$.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
$(N,S)$ and $(N,O)$ determine each other: $O=[S\ne0]$, and if $O=1$ then $S=2$ iff $N=1$. So
$H(N,S)=H(N,O)$ and $I(N;S)-I(N;O)=H(S)-H(O)$. The laws are $P(S=2)=1/n^2$,
$P(S=1)=2(n-1)/n^2$, $P(S=0)=P(O=0)=(n-1)^2/n^2$ and $P(O=1)=(2n-1)/n^2$. Strictness is the strict
subadditivity $\eta(a+b)<\eta(a)+\eta(b)$ for $a,b>0$.
\end{proof}

\begin{corollary}[Degree nine]
For $C_9$: $I_s(9)\approx0.00604$ bits and $I(N;S)=I_s(9)+\eta(\frac1{81})+\eta(\frac{16}{81})-\eta(\frac{17}{81})\approx0.07378$ bits.
The same value holds modulo $19$ by Proposition~\ref{prop:thick}.
\end{corollary}

\section{Additivity of the totient entropy}

\begin{theorem}[Coprime additivity]\label{thm:additive}
If $\gcd(m,n)=1$, then $H_\varphi(mn)=H_\varphi(m)+H_\varphi(n)$.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
$(a,b)\mapsto ab$ is a bijection $\Div(m)\times\Div(n)\to\Div(mn)$, and
$\varphi(ab)/mn=x_ay_b$ with $x_a=\varphi(a)/m$ and $y_b=\varphi(b)/n$. Using
$\eta(xy)=y\eta(x)+x\eta(y)$ and $\sum_ax_a=\sum_by_b=1$,
\[
H_\varphi(mn)=\sum_{a,b}\bigl(y_b\eta(x_a)+x_a\eta(y_b)\bigr)=H_\varphi(m)+H_\varphi(n).\qedhere
\]
\end{proof}

Multiplicativity of $\varphi$ becomes additivity of entropy: the type of a cyclic field of degree
$mn$ is an independent pair of types in its coprime-degree subfields. Coprimality is necessary:
$H_\varphi(9)\approx1.2244\neq2H_\varphi(3)\approx1.8366$ bits.

\begin{proposition}
For a prime $p$, $H_\varphi(p)=\eta(1/p)+\eta((p-1)/p)$.
\end{proposition}

\begin{corollary}[The $\ell=37$ rung]
$H_\varphi(18)=\log2+\tfrac43\log3-\tfrac89\log2$: the rung $\Q(\zeta_{37})^+$ lies exactly one bit
above the degree-nine rung.
\end{corollary}

\section{Algorithms}

\paragraph{Algorithm A (type from residue).} Compute $r=p^2\bmod\ell$ and return the least $f\ge1$
with $r^f\equiv1\pmod\ell$. This costs $O(m)$ multiplications naively, or $O(\log^2m)$ modular
exponentiations using the factorisation of $m$.

\paragraph{Algorithm B (factor-degree pattern).} Distinct-degree factorisation of $\Psi$ over
$\F_p$: for $k=1,2,\dots$ compute $g_k=\gcd(\Psi,x^{p^k}-x)$, record $\deg g_k/k$ factors of
degree $k$, and divide out. The cost is $O(m^3\log p)$ field operations. By
Corollary~\ref{cor:pattern} the output is $[f^{m/f}]$.

\paragraph{Algorithm C (exact mutual information).} Tabulate the counts of $X$, $Y$ and $(X,Y)$
on $\Omega$, and return $H(X)+H(Y)-H(X,Y)$. This takes $O(|\Omega|)$ time.

\section{Numerical evidence}

\begin{itemize}
\item Over $295{,}946$ unramified primes, the frequencies of $f=1,3,9$ in $K_{19}$ matched
$1/9,2/9,6/9$ to within $2\times10^{-4}$, and the empirical entropy matched $1.2244$ bits.
\item The type is constant on every residue class modulo $19$. A control residue modulo a number
coprime to $19$ carries no information about the type.
\item For $400$ random primes, the factor pattern of $\Psi_{19}\bmod p$ agreed with $f(p)$ in all
cases. The root count could not separate $f=3$ from $f=9$.
\item Semiprimes: measured $0.5330$ bits against the exact $0.52650$; which-factor extra measured
$0.00053$ against the exact $0$; split count measured $0.0746$ against the exact $0.07378$.
\end{itemize}

\begin{remark}[Corrections to earlier estimates]
The exact-enumeration value of the semiprime pair information is $0.52650$ bits, not $0.5302$.
The split-count projection ($0.07378$ bits) is not the OR-dial $I_s(9)$, which is $0.00604$ bits;
the two differ by the correction of Theorem~\ref{thm:splitcount}. A Frobenius of order $3$ fixes
no roots (Proposition~\ref{prop:fixed}), so root counts cannot certify types; factor patterns are
required. Encodings of unordered type pairs by a single integer (such as $3\min+\max$) must be
checked for injectivity, since collisions merge cells and bias mutual-information estimates.
\end{remark}

\section{Discussion}
Granting the uniform model, full pinning on every rung reduces to the arithmetic of a cyclic group
of even order: squaring is two-to-one, its image is the $m$-torsion, and there are exactly
$\varphi(d)$ elements of each order $d$. The residue carries all the information about the type,
the sign is pure thickening, and the most natural readout, root counting, fails exactly on
composite degrees. For semiprimes, commutativity makes ``which factor'' unrecoverable, and the
split count differs from the OR-fork by a closed-form entropy correction.

\section{Future work}
\begin{enumerate}
\item Extend the laws to all cyclic and abelian fields of arbitrary conductor.
\item Quantify the pinning gap $H(T)-I(p\bmod c;T)$ for non-abelian Galois groups.
\item Prove semiprime thickening for all $\ell$, and give $I(N;\{T_p,T_q\})$ in closed form in
terms of the divisor lattice of $m$.
\item Study the asymptotics of the additive arithmetic function $H_\varphi$.
\item Replace the uniform model by effective Chebotarev bounds.
\end{enumerate}

